/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Bending is symmetrical when the moment acts about a principal axis of the section, usually an axis of symmetry.
   - The derivation follows from these in four steps:
   - σ is the direct stress (Pa) at distance y (m) from the neutral axis; M is the bending moment (N·m); I is the second moment of area about the neutral axis (m⁴); E is Young's modulus (Pa); R is the radius of curvature of the beam axis (m); Z 
   - Doubling the depth of a rectangular section multiplies I by eight and ymax by two, so the peak stress falls to a quarter.
   - This unit uses one convention throughout: z along the beam, x horizontal and y upwards from the centroid, tension positive.
   - A section not symmetric about its neutral axis, such as a T-section, has two section moduli, Ztop = I/ytop and Zbot = I/ybot.
   - A stretched fibre contracts sideways by ν ε, where ν is Poisson's ratio (about 0.33 for aluminium alloy, 0.3 for steel); a compressed fibre widens.
   - R′ is the transverse (anticlastic) radius of curvature of the cross-section (m); R is the radius of the beam axis (m); ν is Poisson's ratio.
   - For aluminium R′ is about three times R: bend a rubber eraser and its end face stops being rectangular.
   - The curl is free only on a narrow strip.
*/
